<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Albans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Albans is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, beside the site of the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>. It grew around the abbey built at the shrine of Saint Alban, traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first Christian martyr, whose church is now the cathedral. Two battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> were fought there in 1455 and 1461.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-albans.jpg" alt="St Albans" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruth Hartnup from Vancouver, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Albans_market_from_the_Clock_Tower_(164240202).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
